course: biology
lesson: 133
title: Finding Nemo: real blue tangs, sea turtles and reef creatures
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Dory, Crush, Squirt, Bloat and Mr Ray are all based on real sea animals, but the film changes some things for the story.
explain: Real blue tangs do not forget everything: most fish remember things for weeks or months, so Dory's memory is a story idea, not fish science.
explain: Imagine a beach at night: tiny turtles pop out of the sand and race towards the brightest thing they can see, which is normally the moonlit sea; that is why beach towns switch off their lights in hatching season.
explain: Sea turtles cannot pull their heads into their shells like Squirt does, and to a hungry turtle a floating plastic bag looks just like a jellyfish.
fact: Adult blue tangs are blue with black markings and a yellow tail, but young ones are bright yellow, and blue tangs can turn paler at night or when scared.
fact: A blue tang has a sharp spine like a little knife near its tail that it can flick out to defend itself.
fact: Blue tangs often feed in groups, grazing algae off coral and rocks, which helps them push past fish guarding their own algae patches and stops algae smothering the coral.
fact: Green sea turtles probably live 80 years or more, but 150 years is more than scientists know of, and they may not have babies until they are 20 to 40 years old.
fact: Sea turtles have lived in the oceans since the time of the dinosaurs.
fact: The East Australian Current is real: it carries warm water south along the coast of Australia, and turtles and fish use currents to travel with less effort.
fact: Turtle hatchlings climb out of their nests at night and head for the brightest horizon, usually the sea; street and hotel lights can lead them the wrong way.
fact: Sea turtles cannot pull their heads or flippers into their shells, because their streamlined swimming shells have no room inside.
fact: Sea turtles eat jellyfish, and floating plastic bags look like jellyfish, so turtles sometimes swallow plastic, which can block their stomachs.
fact: Leatherback turtles eat huge amounts of jellyfish, and their throats are lined with spikes that keep the slippery food from sliding back out.
fact: Jellyfish sting with tiny harpoon cells in their tentacles that fire when touched, and they have no brain, heart or bones and are mostly water.
fact: Pufferfish gulp water and swell into a spiky ball when scared, which makes them hard to swallow, and many contain a strong poison, so in Japan only specially trained cooks may prepare them.
fact: Spotted eagle rays flap wing-like fins to fly through the water and sometimes leap out of it; rays are flat relatives of sharks with skeletons of cartilage (the bendy stuff in your ear), not bone.
exercise: Do real blue tangs forget everything at once? | No, most fish remember things for weeks or months | Yes, they forget everything after about three seconds | Yes, all reef fish have no memory | No, they remember every single thing for a hundred years
exercise: What colour is a young blue tang? | Bright yellow | Bright blue all over | Orange with white stripes | Black all over
exercise: How does a blue tang defend itself? | With a sharp spine near its tail | With a poisonous bite from its sharp front teeth | By blowing up like a balloon | By squirting ink
exercise: What do blue tangs eat as they graze in groups? | Algae growing on coral and rocks | Small sharks that swim near the reef | The eggs that sea turtles lay on the beach | Sand and pebbles
exercise: Why are grazing tangs good for a reef? | They stop algae from smothering the coral | They eat all the coral so that it grows back stronger | They make the water warmer | They keep turtles away from the reef
exercise: How long do green sea turtles probably live? | 80 years or more | Only about 2 years | Only about 10 years | Over 500 years, longer than any tree
exercise: At what age may a green sea turtle first have babies? | About 20 to 40 years | About 1 year | About 100 years | About 3 months
exercise: Is the East Australian Current real? | Yes, it carries warm water south along Australia's coast | No, it was made up for the film, like the talking fish | Yes, but it flows only on land | No, oceans have no currents
exercise: Why do sea animals ride ocean currents? | To travel far easily | To find fresh water to drink | To keep away from the sun | Because they cannot swim at all
exercise: How do turtle hatchlings find the sea at night? | They head for the brightest horizon, usually the sea | They follow their mother back down the beach to the waves | They smell the salt from the nest | They listen for seagulls
exercise: Why do some beaches turn off their lights in hatching season? | Lights can lead the hatchlings the wrong way | Lights wake the turtle mothers sleeping in the sea | Lights make the sand too hot | Lights scare away the jellyfish
exercise: Can a sea turtle pull its head into its shell? | No, its shell has no room | Yes, just like a garden tortoise does | Yes, but only when it is asleep | Only the baby sea turtles can do it
exercise: Why do sea turtles sometimes swallow plastic bags? | Floating bags look like jellyfish | Plastic tastes sweet to them, like fruit | They use the bags to build their nests | They think the bags are seaweed beds
exercise: What do a leatherback's throat spikes do? | Keep slippery jellyfish from sliding back out | Chew up the hard shells of crabs and lobsters | Help it breathe under water | Scare away sharks
exercise: How does a jellyfish sting? | Tiny harpoon cells in its tentacles fire when touched | It bites with rows of sharp little teeth under its bell | It sprays poison from its head | It burns with electricity
exercise: Why does a pufferfish blow up when scared? | A big round spiky ball is hard to swallow | It is floating up to breathe air | It is showing off to the other pufferfish nearby | It is storing food for later
exercise: How does a spotted eagle ray swim? | By flapping wing-like fins as if flying | By wiggling small legs hidden under its flat body | By shooting out jets of water | By rolling along the sea floor
exercise: What is a ray's skeleton made of? | Cartilage, like the soft part of your ear | Hard bone, just like the skeleton of a cat or a cod | Shell, like a crab's | It has no skeleton at all
test: «4 turtles riding the current, how many flippers is that» | 16 | 8 | 4 | 20
test: «Crush says he's 150 lol is that legit» What is right? | Green turtles may live 80 years or more, but 150 is beyond what scientists know | Yes, all turtles live to exactly 150 and then go back to the beach where they hatched | No, turtles live only 5 years | Yes, and some live to 1,000
test: «Squirt hides in his shell when scared, all turtles do it» What is right? | Sea turtles cannot pull their heads in at all | Yes, every turtle hides its head when a shark swims by | Yes, but only the babies | No, only their tails go in
test: In Tom and Jerry, Tom gets stung by a jellyfish and lights up like a bulb. What really happens with a jellyfish sting? | Harpoon cells fire a painful sting into the skin; nothing lights up | The sting gives you a burst of electric power, so you glow for a few minutes | The sting turns your skin see-through | Nothing, jellyfish never sting people
test: «Dory's blue so the yellow one at the aquarium can't be a blue tang, duh» What is right? | Young blue tangs are bright yellow before they turn blue | Right, blue tangs are bright blue from the day they hatch | It must be a sick blue tang | Yellow means it is a clownfish
test: What would happen if a beach hotel kept its bright lights on all night in hatching season? | Hatchlings could crawl towards the lights instead of the sea | The hatchlings would find the sea faster, as the lights show them the way | The turtles would stay in the nest for a year | Nothing would change at all
test: «chucked my plastic bag in the sea, it'll just float, no biggie» Why is it a problem? | A turtle may take it for a jellyfish, eat it and get its stomach blocked | It will turn into a jellyfish after a few weeks in salty water | It will help fish build nests | The sea dissolves it in a day
test: «got stung on the beach, jellyfish bit me with its teeth» What is right? | Jellyfish have no teeth; tiny harpoon cells in the tentacles stung you | Yes, jellyfish have rows of tiny sharp teeth under their soft bell | Yes, they bite with their heads | No, jellyfish only scratch
test: «pufferfish are cute, gonna cook one for tea» What is the best answer? | No, many contain a strong poison; in Japan only trained cooks prepare them | Fine, they are completely safe to eat once they have blown up | Fine, if you boil it twice | Fine, the poison is only in the spines
test: «ray's just a flat fish with bones like a cod» What is right? | Rays are flat shark relatives with skeletons of cartilage | Yes, rays have exactly the same hard bones as cod and other fish | Rays are a kind of turtle | Rays have no skeleton at all
test: «why do blue tangs gang up in big groups, weird» What is right? | Together they can push past fish guarding their own algae patches | They are all lost like Dory and are looking for their families on the reef | They are scared of the dark | They are waiting for a current
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the memory of the blue tang. | No particular deficiency of memory is known; most fish retain information for weeks or months | The species is incapable of retaining any information beyond several seconds | The species retains no information whatsoever after hatching | The species retains every event of its life without exception
exam: Identify the principal reason for which artificial illumination is reduced upon certain beaches during the hatching season of sea turtles. | Hatchlings orient towards the brightest horizon and may be drawn away from the sea by such lights | Illumination elevates the temperature of the sand to a degree that is harmful to the eggs beneath it | Illumination causes adult turtles to abandon their nests | Illumination attracts jellyfish which prey upon hatchlings
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capacity of a sea turtle to withdraw into its shell? | It is unable to withdraw either its head or its flippers into the shell | It is able to withdraw its head but not its flippers | It is able to withdraw its whole body as a land tortoise does | It is able to withdraw its head only during sleep
exam: Ascertain the reason for which sea turtles are observed to ingest floating plastic bags. | Such bags resemble jellyfish, which form part of the diet of sea turtles | Such bags possess a sweet taste attractive to sea turtles | Sea turtles employ such bags in the construction of nests | Sea turtles mistake such bags for beds of seaweed
